  "output": "DETAILED TECHNICAL ANALYSIS OF QUEUE INCIDENT\n\nThe incident timeline establishes a clear causal chain. At 09:00, the system operated normally with queue depth of 120 tasks and 8 workers. At 09:10, queue depth spiked to 1,850 over a ten-minute window with zero worker scaling. This rapid accumulation immediately preceded a task latency spike from 1.8 to 14.2 seconds at 09:12, indicating degraded throughput rather than a processing capacity breach.\n\nHost metrics at 09:14 revealed no resource exhaustion: CPU was 43%, memory 51%, disk 22%. These levels indicate the system possessed ample idle capacity for task execution. The bottleneck was therefore not local resource saturation but rather external or dependency-driven.\n\nAt 09:15, evidence emerged that dependency B began rate limiting, rejecting 31% of outbound requests with HTTP 429 responses. This rate-limiting event provides the single most plausible explanation for the queue backup. When 31% of requests are rejected, tasks cannot complete and must either retry or fail. E06 shows that the system's retry policy responded aggressively: retry volume increased 420% at 09:16, employing exponential backoff with a maximum of three retries per task.\n\nThis retry configuration acted as an amplifier of the initial rate-limiting problem. Each rejected request was retried up to three times, multiplying the load sent back to the already-constrained dependency. A 420% retry volume increase against a dependency that was already rate-limiting at 31% rejection created a reinforcing failure loop: more retries hit the dependency, more requests were rejected, more tasks entered retry queues, and upstream concurrency remained high.\n\nThe worker scaling intervention at 09:20 provides critical evidence of root-cause differentiation. The system was scaled from 8 to 16 workers—a 100% increase—yet queue depth fell only 6% over ten minutes. If the problem had been insufficient processing capacity, doubling worker count should have yielded dramatic queue reduction. Instead, minimal improvement occurred. This strongly indicates workers were I/O bound, not CPU bound. Each additional worker added more concurrent load against a rate-limited upstream service, exacerbating the problem rather than solving it.\n\nAt 09:26, the system operators performed a critical experiment: they temporarily disabled retries for dependency B. This change caused queue depth to fall 38% in six minutes while the direct failure rate increased. This experiment isolates the retry amplifier. By removing retries, tasks that failed on dependency B now failed immediately rather than re-entering the queue for retry attempts. The queue could drain faster. This confirms that the retry storm was not a symptom of the primary problem but rather a secondary amplification mechanism.\n\nFinal resolution came at 09:35 when dependency B's rate limits were raised. HTTP 429 responses fell below 1%, and queue depth returned below 150 within eleven minutes. This definitively establishes that the rate limit on dependency B was the primary causal agent. Once the upstream service could accept the offered load without rate limiting, the queue naturally drained despite the unchanged retry configuration.\n\nE10 excludes several potential confounders: no deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred during the incident window. This rules out scenarios in which infrastructure changes triggered the event.\n\nIn summary, the primary cause was rate limiting on external dependency B. The amplifier was an aggressive retry policy that multiplied rejected requests. Worker scaling failed because the bottleneck was upstream I/O rate limiting, not local compute capacity. The retry policy was inappropriate for the failure mode: exponential backoff with three retries is reasonable for transient failures, but against deliberate rate limiting, it only intensified load on the constrained resource. Resolution required addressing the root cause (upstream rate limit) rather than treating symptoms (queue depth) with scaling or retry tuning alone.",
